THE FINAL RUN DOWN!

Week 1
This week, I began developing my final project, it does not have a name yet, but it's going to be an interactive collage game inspired by the game called "Exquisite Corpse", most people know it in french "Le Cadavre Exquis". I was trying to figure out what to do for this final project, and because of my past experience coding; I knew that sometimes it is better to do something simple that works well instead of being overly ambicious and making something overly complicated that will make the end result not be as good.

The idea came to me beacuse I knew I wanted to make something playful and interactive. At the start to the semester in one of my other classes some classmates and I had to create a workshop for the rest of the class and we decided to have the "Le Cadavre Exquis" as the first activity. It was my first time experiencing this game and I thought it was so funny and crative, so I thought "mmmm why not make this into a virtual game" and maybe play around with it in order ro be a bit cotroversial through satire. So the idea is to create an experience in which users assemble characters by selecting different body parts that are falling across the screen. 

I started by collecting different images and editing them in order to have three different categories, at first I thought on having four (head, chest, legs, and shoes), but after I figured that it would be easier to find and edit images if I combined the legs and shoes. Something that I wanted to challenge myself with was the use of arrays and loops, since these were concepts that I understood in theory but I still srugggle with. I had some problems at the beginning figuring out how to load the images becaause I had so many, here is where I found how to use the preload() function, which allows me to load all my images before the sketch starts running. This is going to be particularly useful for my game because the images are the main elements and by preloading them I am making sure that they are available before the animation starts; and avoid problems like images not appearing or their dimensions being wrong etc. Also, considering that I have 19 images (im still figuring if to add more), I figure this was a good opportunity for me in where I could use a loop to go through the number of filenames and store the images in their corresponding arrays. 

After getting the images to appear, I started working in the animation. I worked with one of my prototypes as inspiration, it was the Week 7-Arrays, the one with the falling stars. Same as the starts I wated the body parts to fall continuously, here the use of parameters was key; so instead of having to repeat the same code, I used the same function and gave it different information depending on which image I wanted to animate. It took me a long time, because it was trial and error to make it work sometimes I encouter silly errors like misspelling words or forgetting to call the function in draw(), but by having my own prototypes as examples to follow was really helpful. 

Then I added the tarot card as the main space where the users are going to assemble the pieces. I think this is where I am trying more to diciffer the visual aspect of the game, because I thought on adding a tarot card because it could be interesting considering that tarot cards are associated with interpretations and predictions, and I could play around with this idea when giving the user a veredict about the character they created. 

The last thing I tried to work on was the mouse interaction. I started experimention with collision detection to identify wheter the user is clicking on a specific image. Here I used a lot on console.log to test wheter the different parts of my code whre working. First to check if the program was recognizing the mouse clicks, then to identify the coordinates of each click, and finally to see which image the user was selecting. So, here is where I am; I will keep working on the mousePressed and hopefully not die in the process.
